{% set local_problem %}
<p>New Hope's rolling terrain creates both opportunity and challenge for drainage. Water wants to flow downhill — the question is whether it flows away from your home or toward it.</p>

<p>Surface extensions only move water a few feet, then release it. On sloped lots, that water follows gravity, sometimes ending up right back near your foundation or creating erosion channels across your yard.</p>

<p>Underground drains take control. They capture roof runoff at your downspouts and route it through buried pipes to a specific exit point — the bottom of your lot, a drainage area, or far from anything you need to protect.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes connecting your downspouts to a discharge point. Water flows through a sealed channel, exiting through pop-up emitters or at a daylight point.</p>

<p>We design systems specifically for your property — routing around obstacles, working with your terrain's slope, and depositing water at logical exit points.</p>

<p>For New Hope properties, this often means using the natural slope to advantage, routing water downhill through controlled paths rather than letting it find its own way.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been installing underground drainage throughout eastern Madison County for over 20 years. We understand sloped terrain, varied soil conditions, and the importance of working with your property's natural features.</p>

<p>Our systems last decades. Proper materials, careful installation, and smart design mean your drainage problems get solved permanently.</p>
{% endset %}

